    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the controller's established validation-helper idiom and I'd expect a maintainer of this codebase to find it natural.\n\n- **Right unit.** The controller already owns a family of private request-shaping helpers (`normalizeTelephone`, `validateNationalNumberLength`, `normalizeEmail`, `normalizeAddress`) each invoked inline in `addOwner`. `validatePostcode(owner.getCity(), owner.getPostcode())` is added in the same normalize/validate block (line 118) and defined in the same region of the file, following the identical shape: a `private static` method that throws `ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, ...)` and is fronted by a constant lookup table with thorough Javadoc \u2014 exactly mirroring `COUNTRY_NATIONAL_LENGTHS` + `validateNationalNumberLength`.\n\n- **No duplication.** The city\u2192region mapping is not re-declared; it reuses `OwnerMapper.CITY_REGION`, the existing single source of truth already driving `toLocality`. Only the genuinely new fact \u2014 the region\u2192postcode-range table \u2014 is introduced, and it's a validation concern so it belongs beside the other controller validation constants (`EMAIL_PATTERN`, `ADDRESS_ABBREVIATIONS`, `COUNTRY_NATIONAL_LENGTHS`) rather than in the mapper.\n\n- **No catch-all growth.** The model/schema/OpenAPI additions are ordinary parallel additions to existing `email`/`registration_date` fields across all four DB dialects and the DTO contract, consistent with how other optional fields are declared. Nothing is bolted onto an unrelated class.\n\nThe early-return null handling and the delegation of 4-digit format enforcement to request validation (OpenAPI `pattern`) keep the method single-purpose. Structurally clean; no changes needed.",
